diagnosis_text:
A thorough seven-probe sweep across all network layers found no fault. Phase 1 (get_reachability) confirmed the L3 path from host_1_1_1_1 to the DNS server (10.200.0.2) and all web servers is fully intact with 0% packet loss. Phase A (infra_sweep, l2_snapshot) found no ACL rules, no static ARP poisoning, no duplicate MACs, and no resolver/gateway divergence between hosts — both client hosts use 10.200.0.2 as their nameserver. Phase B (ospf_snapshot, tc_snapshot) confirmed all six FRR routers have healthy OSPF adjacencies (Full state) and zero traffic-shaping qdiscs anywhere on the fabric. Phase D (service_snapshot, pressure_sweep) directly falsified the user's complaint: host_1_1_1_1 resolves web0.local through web3.local to the correct IP addresses, receives HTTP 200 responses from all four web servers in under 7 ms, and shows no CPU or socket pressure. The DNS daemon on 10.200.0.2 is reachable and returning correct answers. There is no flap script, no port-53 ACL, no daemon crash trace, and no historical evidence of any transient failure. The root cause is that no fault exists in the current lab state — the reported DNS outage is unconfirmed and contradicted by live diagnostic evidence. No device needs restarting.
=== END ===